---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { SECURITY, REPO, INSTALLER_SHA256, LATEST_INSTALLER_NAME } from "../links";
---
<Base title="Security" description="What whereti installs, what each part may do, how to check the installer, and how to report a problem." prose>
  <h1>Security</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    whereti sees every file name on your PC, so it is built to keep that to itself. Here is what runs, what it is allowed
    to do, and how to check what you downloaded.
  </p>

  <h2 id="parts">What gets installed</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Runs as</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>whereti.exe</td><td>You</td><td>The search window and the icon next to the clock.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>whereti-service.exe</td><td>Windows service</td><td>Reads the drives' file tables, keeps the index, answers searches from the window.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>whereti-extract.exe</td><td>A separate process</td><td>Reads the text out of one document at a time, with limits on time and memory.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>wt.exe</td><td>You</td><td>The same search from a command line, for those who want it.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Everything goes to <code>C:\Program Files\whereti</code>. The service talks to the window only through a local pipe
    on the same PC.
  </p>

  <h2 id="design">How it is kept safe</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Almost no network.</strong> The service, the document reader and the installer never connect to anything.
      The search window asks for updates at most once a day, sending only its version number.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Updates are checked on your PC.</strong> An update is installed only if the installer carries a valid
      signature from Software Hive S.R.L. and matches the checksum published with the release. Someone who took over the
      update server could stop updates, but could not make whereti install anything.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The index is locked.</strong> It lives in <code>%ProgramData%\whereti</code>, readable only by Windows and
      administrators. Other users and ordinary programs cannot list your files through it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Documents are read apart.</strong> PDF and Office files are complex, and a damaged or crafted one can crash
      the program reading it. whereti reads them in a separate short-lived process with time and memory limits, so the
      worst case is one document skipped.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Nothing runs from your files.</strong> whereti reads documents as text. It never runs macros or scripts in them.</li>
    <li><strong>Written in Rust,</strong> which rules out whole families of memory bugs.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="checking-the-download">Checking the download</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Right-click the installer, choose Properties, then the Digital Signatures tab.</li>
    <li>The signer must be <strong>Software Hive S.R.L.</strong>, and Details must say the signature is OK.</li>
    <li>When you run it, Windows shows the same name as the verified publisher.</li>
  </ol>
  {INSTALLER_SHA256 && (
    <p>
      You can also compare the SHA-256 of {LATEST_INSTALLER_NAME}. In PowerShell:
      <code>Get-FileHash .\{LATEST_INSTALLER_NAME}</code>. It must be:
      <span class="hash">{INSTALLER_SHA256}</span>
    </p>
  )}
  <p>Only download whereti from whereti.com or from <a href={`${REPO}/releases`}>its GitHub releases</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="limits">What this does not protect against</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      An administrator account on the same PC can read the index, as it can read your files anyway.
    </li>
    <li>Malware already running as administrator can do anything, with or without whereti.</li>
    <li>
      There has been no independent security audit yet. The code is open for anyone to review.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="reporting">Reporting a problem</h2>
  <p>
    Write to <a href={`mailto:${SECURITY}`}>{SECURITY}</a> with what you found and how to reproduce it. You will get a
    reply within a few days. Please give us a reasonable time to fix it before making it public. We will credit you if
    you want.
  </p>
</Base>
